.TH ISING_PARALLEL 1 "November 2026" "ising_parallel 2.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ising_parallel \- multithreaded Monte Carlo simulation of the 2-D Ising and
q-state Potts models with live SDL2 visualisation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ising_parallel
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ising_parallel \-\-batch
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ising_parallel
simulates the two-dimensional ferromagnetic Ising model and the q-state Potts
model on an
.IR L \(mu L
square lattice with periodic boundary conditions, using either the
Metropolis single-site algorithm (parallelised over all cores with a
two-colour checkerboard decomposition) or the Wolff single-cluster algorithm.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-batch ,
an SDL2 window shows the spin configuration on the left and a live panel on
the right with temperature, the order parameter
.IR m ,
energy per spin
.IR e ,
susceptibility \(*x, specific heat
.IR C\s-2v\s+2 ,
jackknife error bars, the Binder cumulant, the integrated autocorrelation time \(*t of
.IR m ,
exact Onsager reference values (Ising), history plots and a key legend.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-batch
no window is opened: the program thermalises, measures for the requested
number of sweeps, prints one line of results (optionally CSV) and exits.
.PP
Units are
.IR J " = " k\s-2B\s+2 " = 1;"
temperatures are in units of
.IR J / k\s-2B\s+2 .
The exact critical temperatures are
.IR T\s-2c\s+2 " = 2/ln(1+\(sr2) \(~~ 2.26919"
for the Ising model and
.IR T\s-2c\s+2 " = 1/ln(1+\(srq)"
for the q-state Potts model.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-L\ N ", " \-\-size= N
Lattice side length (even, default 512).
.TP
.BI \-j\ N ", " \-\-threads= N
Number of worker threads (default: number of online CPUs). The main thread
is one of the workers; do not exceed the number of physical cores.
.TP
.BI \-m\ NAME ", " \-\-model= NAME
.B ising
(default) or
.BR potts .
.TP
.BI \-q\ Q ", " \-\-states= Q
Number of Potts states, 2..16 (default 3). Ignored for the Ising model.
.TP
.BI \-T\ T ", " \-\-temp= T
Temperature. Default: 1.5
.I T\s-2c\s+2
in the GUI,
.I T\s-2c\s+2
in batch mode.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-wolff
Use the Wolff cluster algorithm instead of Metropolis.
.TP
.BI \-s\ S ", " \-\-seed= S
RNG seed (64-bit, decimal or 0x-hex). Default: derived from time and PID.
Runs with the same seed, lattice size and thread count are bit-reproducible.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-batch
Headless mode.
.TP
.BI \-n\ N ", " \-\-sweeps= N
Measurement sweeps in batch mode (default 10000).
.TP
.BI \-t\ N ", " \-\-therm= N
Thermalisation sweeps before measuring in batch mode (default 1000).
.TP
.BI \-\-scan\  A:B:K
Batch mode: run
.I K
temperatures from
.I A
to
.I B
inclusive, carrying the configuration from one temperature to the next.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Batch mode: comma-separated output with a header line.
.TP
.BI \-\-frames\  N
GUI: exit after
.I N
frames (for benchmarking and testing).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B scroll wheel, Up, Down
Temperature by
.IR T\s-2c\s+2 /50 .
Hold Shift for steps of
.IR T\s-2c\s+2 /500 .
.TP
.B + / \-
Sweeps per frame (1..200).
.TP
.B W
Toggle Wolff / Metropolis.
.TP
.B M
Toggle Ising / Potts (lattice re-randomised,
.I T/T\s-2c\s+2
preserved).
.TP
.B [ / ]
Decrease / increase Potts
.IR q .
.TP
.B C
Cycle colour mode: spin; spins tinted by the owning thread (shows the row-band decomposition); local energy.
.TP
.B Space
Pause.
.TP
.B H / K
Hot (random) / cold (aligned) start.
.TP
.B R
Reset statistics window.
.TP
.B P
Save a screenshot
.RI ( ising_YYYYmmdd_HHMMSS.bmp ).
.TP
.B V
Start/stop MP4 recording through an
.BR ffmpeg (1)
pipe.
.TP
.B Q, Esc
Quit.
.SH BATCH OUTPUT
One line per temperature with the columns
.IR "model, q, L, threads, alg, T, Tc, sweeps, m, m_err, e, e_err, chi, chi_err, cv, cv_err, U, U_err, tau_m, m_exact, e_exact, avg_cluster, secs, sweeps_per_s" .
.I U
is the Binder cumulant 1 \- <m^4>/(3<m^2>^2), whose curves for different
.I L
cross at
.IR T\s-2c\s+2
(at 0.6107 for the 2-D Ising model); the accompanying
.B binder.sh
script automates the scan.
Errors are jackknife estimates over 20 blocks;
.I tau_m
is the integrated autocorrelation time of
.I m
in sweeps, estimated from the blocking ratio.
.I m_exact
and
.I e_exact
are the Onsager infinite-lattice values (Ising only; NaN for Potts).
Finite lattices show systematic shifts near
.IR T\s-2c\s+2 ,
of order
.IR 1/L
in the Ising energy.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ISING_FONT
Path to a TrueType font for the panel. Otherwise common DejaVu, Liberation,
Ubuntu and macOS system fonts are tried; without a font the simulation runs
with the panel text disabled.
.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive Potts model with 5 states on a 1024\(mu1024 lattice, 8 threads:
.PP
.RS
ising_parallel \-m potts \-q 5 \-L 1024 \-j 8
.RE
.PP
Check the Ising magnetisation at T = 2.0 against the exact value:
.PP
.RS
ising_parallel \-\-batch \-L 64 \-T 2.0 \-n 20000
.RE
.PP
Susceptibility scan through the 3-state Potts transition as CSV:
.PP
.RS
ising_parallel \-\-batch \-m potts \-q 3 \-L 128 \-w \-\-scan 0.90:1.10:21 \-\-csv > scan.csv
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a runtime error (no display, out of memory), 2 on a
command-line error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1)
.PP
L. Onsager, Phys. Rev. 65, 117 (1944).
U. Wolff, Phys. Rev. Lett. 62, 361 (1989).
F. Y. Wu, Rev. Mod. Phys. 54, 235 (1982).
.SH AUTHOR
Mico Mrkaic.
